source: crates/escher-cli/tests/cli/usage.rs
expression: trimmed
---
status: Success
--- stdout
Render the tiling of a signature in orbifold notation and write it to a PNG file

Usage: escher render [OPTIONS] --output <FILE> <SIGNATURE>

Arguments:
  <SIGNATURE>
          The signature in orbifold notation, such as *632. In a POSIX shell, put a signature that contains *, (, or ) in single quotes, as in '2*(12)'

Options:
  -o, --output <FILE>
          The PNG file to write. An existing file is replaced

      --size <WIDTHxHEIGHT>
          The image size in pixels, as WIDTHxHEIGHT

          [default: 512x512]

      --colouring <COLOURING>
          One colour for every tile, parity for the colouring with two colours in which no two neighbouring tiles share a colour, or the id of a colouring that the command info --colourings lists, such as 4a. The default is parity when the tiling has it, and one otherwise

      --colours <COLOURS>
          The fill colours, separated by commas, each as #rgb or #rrggbb, one for each colour of the colouring. The default takes the first colours of a built-in list of 12. In a POSIX shell, put the colours in single quotes, as in '#e9c46a,#2a9d8f'

      --border-width <WIDTH>
          The border width, in the unit of --border-unit. 0 draws no border. The default is 1 for pixels and 0.05 for intrinsic

      --border-unit <UNIT>
          The unit of --border-width: pixels of the image, or intrinsic for a length in the geometry of the tiling, such as 0.05

          [default: pixels]
          [possible values: pixels, intrinsic]

      --border-colour <COLOUR>
          The border colour, as #rgb or #rrggbb

          [default: #000000]

      --samples <N>
          The number of samples along each side of a pixel. The pixel averages N by N samples

          [default: 4]

      --max-folds <N>
          The largest number of folds of one sample. A sample that needs more takes the border colour

          [default: 1500]

      --background <COLOUR>
          The colour outside the model of a hyperbolic tiling or the ball of a spherical tiling, as #rgb or #rrggbb

          [default: #ffffff]

      --threads <N>
          The number of threads, from 1 to 256. The default is the available parallelism, at most 256

      --stats
          Print statistics of the rendering to standard output

      --set <NAME=VALUE>
          Set a shape parameter, as NAME=VALUE, such as aspect=1.5.

          A shape parameter is a number that changes the shape of the tiles without
          changing their symmetry. Which parameters exist depends on the signature:

            aspect
                The ratio of the sides of the rectangle or the parallelogram, for
                *2222, 2222, o, **, xx, *x, 22*, 22x, and 2*22.
            skew
                The shear of the parallelogram of o and of the two rectangles of 2222.
            cornerpair<k>.length
                For a hyperbolic reflection polygon *p1...pn with 4 to 8 corners, the
                length of the side between the two right angles of its k-th pair of
                adjacent corners of order 2.
            slice<i>.length
                For the same polygons, the length of the i-th cut across the polygon,
                a common perpendicular of two of its sides. A polygon with n corners
                has n - 3 lengths, corner pairs first.

          Every other signature has no shape parameter. escher info <SIGNATURE>
          prints one Parameter line for each parameter of the signature, with its
          range and default.

          Give the option once for each parameter, as in:

            escher render o --set aspect=2 --set skew=0.25 -o /tmp/torus.png
            escher render '*3333' --set slice1.length=2 -o /tmp/square.png

      --model <MODEL>
          The model in which a hyperbolic tiling is drawn: the Poincare disk, the upper half-plane, the band, or the Klein disk

          [default: disk]
          [possible values: disk, half-plane, band, klein]

      --rotation <YAW,PITCH,ROLL>
          The rotation of the ball of a spherical tiling, as three angles in degrees. YAW turns the front of the ball to the right, PITCH turns it up, and ROLL turns the image counterclockwise, in this order

          [default: 0,0,0]

      --light <X,Y,Z>
          The direction from the ball of a spherical tiling to the light, as X,Y,Z, with X to the right, Y up, and Z toward the viewer. The length is ignored

          [default: -1,1,2]

      --shading <SHADING>
          Phong shading of the ball of a spherical tiling, or none

          [default: phong]
          [possible values: phong, none]

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')
--- stderr
--- end
